  <div class="story">
    <div><h3>The problem</h3><p>A remittance is cash. Once it is picked up, the person who earned it cannot tell whether it paid for the prescription or for something else, and every transfer loses a slice to fees and exchange windows.</p><p>Families asked for something narrower: a way to pay for the medicine, the phone line or the pet food directly.</p></div>
    <div><h3>What I built</h3><p>I built the whole product alone. A Rails app serves the landing page, the checkout, a portal where merchants redeem cards and see settlements, and an admin for refunds, holds and payouts. A REST API lets a merchant redeem from their own till software.</p><p>Two native apps, built with Expo, cover buying, claiming, topping up and showing the code at the counter.</p></div>
    <div><h3>What made it hard</h3><p>It is other people's money, so every cent needs an owner at every moment. A card can be topped up by several senders, partly redeemed, refunded or held, and the merchant must still be paid the right net amount.</p><p>The code shown at the till also had to be safe on a shared screen, so it expires in 90 seconds and can only be charged once.</p></div>
  </div>

