    <div class="eng-notes">
      <div><h4>A ledger that checks itself every night</h4><p>Every purchase, redemption, refund and payout is a typed ledger row tied to the card and the load it came from. A reconcile job re-derives each card's balance from those rows nightly, sorts findings into drift, warning or info, and emails me on any drift.</p></div>
      <div><h4>A redemption can only happen once</h4><p>The till sends an idempotency key with each charge. A repeated key returns the first answer word for word, declines included. The capture locks the token, then the card, then its loads, always in that order, and draws the amount from the oldest load first.</p></div>
      <div><h4>Codes that expire in 90 seconds</h4><p>The QR code is a random 10-character token. The database keeps only its SHA-256 digest; the readable value lives in Redis until it expires. The app asks for a new one when fewer than 15 seconds remain, so a photo of the screen is useless a minute later.</p></div>
      <div><h4>The website moves like the native app</h4><p>The landing page rebuilds the real app screens in HTML and CSS. I solved the app's spring equation directly (damping 18, stiffness 240, mass 0.6 for the tab indicator) so the tab bar on the web settles on the same frame as on the phone.</p></div>
